<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karnak</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karnak is a vast temple complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, dedicated chiefly to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. It was greatly expanded during the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, including a major building programme under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, and functioned as a religious and economic centre. An Egyptian version of the peace treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> is preserved there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: René Hourdry · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
